From 56b9b3b799cecc8c2aee60a60c388adbe3497969 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 18:06:08 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 20/44] fix(chexc): drop the dead THROWS/CAPTURES tokens,
 restoring upstream ordinals

Both token kinds were never produced: 'throws' and 'captures' are soft keywords
recognized by spelling at the few positions a clause may start (SeeingChexcClause),
and the lexer's keyword map never mapped them -- LookupKeyword covers the range
before IDENTIFIER plus a hand-written list neither was in. They sat in the
experimental block of the token table, which is followed by ILLEGAL, DOUBLE_COLON
and FEATURES, so their only effect was to shift those three ordinals by +2:
DOUBLE_COLON became 165 where upstream and every golden say 163.

That broke macro token round-trips, which serialize kinds as integers: 4
Macro/annotation cases, Macro/expr/quote_expr, and the 3 unittest power-assert
invalid_syntax cases, whose macro-call echo vanished from the diagnostics. All 8
pass again, and Tokens.inc is now byte-identical to upstream.

The lexer's chexcEnabled flag goes with them: it was only ever written, never
read, left from a design where 'throws' would have been a real keyword.
Parser::SetChexcEnabled keeps the parser-side flag, which is what
SeeingChexcClause consults.

Found by the first full-tree LLT run of this branch; the compiler/-only gates
could not see it, since the damage was entirely in what other components make of
the token table.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
